@w.lang.en.reply.role core.slot of=w.lang.en.reply key=role many=true kind=atom core.source="hand-core 1"
@w.lang.en.reply.text core.slot of=w.lang.en.reply key=text required=true kind=string core.source="hand-core 1"
@w.lang.en.reply.example core.slot of=w.lang.en.reply key=example kind=string core.source="hand-core 1"
# A discourse template (blocks 14:45 item 2, 15:00 item 4, 17:05): one sentence that makes a rhetorical move
# (`move`, a `k.genres.move name`) in a register (`register`, a `k.genres.register name`: scientific, presentation,
# essay, narrative, report), for the library's std.compose. Several templates of one move and register are
# alternatives (a composer picks among them). `text` is in the library's template notation (`{slot}`,
# `{slot:num=pl}`; the article `a` takes its form from the next word); `slot` (many) lists its slots, each a
# `w.lang.en.slot`. The words outside the slots follow the register of k.genres: its persons (I, we, you), no direct
# question where it has none (an indirect one instead), no contraction where it has none, the past in a narrative.
# How an attribute of the catalog is said (block 00:05 item 2): a template per form of k.attributes.narration
# (`form`: relation, measure, text, count, kind, state; `polarity` yes or no for a state), or per key (`key`, and
# `of` the type when the key is said otherwise in other types); `text` in the library's template notation with the
# slots `role` names ({thing} the thing with its determiner, {value} the value as said) and {key} the key's word;
# `frame` the frame of the set acts.state that reads the sentence back (tests/w.lang.en.phrasing.test.mjs).
@w.lang.en.phrasing core.type of=w.lang.en.phrasing shared=true closed=true core.source="hand-core 1"
